  <h2 id="what">What myleet is</h2>
  <p>myleet is a teaching-first practice app for software engineering internship, co-op and new-grad coding interviews. You read a short note on a pattern, solve problems in the browser against real Python tests, and the app decides what comes back and when.</p>
  <p>Unlike a fixed list, each day is built from your own history: reviews that are due, techniques you keep missing, new problems in the order the book teaches them, and the company sets you pinned. Nothing needs installing, and nothing needs an account.</p>

  <h2 id="ladder">The review ladder</h2>
  <p>Every problem you solve goes on a ladder of growing gaps: 1, 3, 7, 14 and 30 days. When it comes back, a clean solve moves it up one rung.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Climbs one rung:</strong> green tests, no hint or peek at the solution, and under the time target (15, 25 or 45 minutes by difficulty).</li>
    <li><strong>Falls to 1 day:</strong> a hint, a look at the solution, going over time, or giving up. The miss is recorded, not hidden.</li>
    <li><strong>Same day doesn't count:</strong> passing again the same day keeps the rung. Memory only shows across a gap.</li>
    <li><strong>Mastered at 14 days:</strong> a clean cold redo two weeks out. After that the problem visits once a month to stay sharp.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="curriculum">The curriculum</h2>
  <p>The problems are organised by interview pattern, not by textbook chapter: arrays and hashing, two pointers, sliding window, binary search, trees, graphs, dynamic programming and the rest, in eighteen modules. Each module's notes are written as one book, read in order, that derive each technique from the brute force before drilling it. Start with the five <a href={ROUTES.learn}>start-here notes</a> on Learn.</p>
  <p>Problem statements belong to their original sources. myleet links to them and shows them for practice; it doesn't claim them.</p>

  <h2 id="companies">Company sets</h2>
  <p>Each company set is a curated practice plan: problems from the techniques that company's loops tend to lean on, most common first. They are not lists of actual or leaked interview questions, and no one should treat them as such.</p>

  <h2 id="free">Free and open source</h2>
  <p>myleet is free, has no ads and is open source under the MIT license. The code, the curriculum and the trainer live at <a href="https://github.com/kidskoding/myleet" rel="noopener">github.com/kidskoding/myleet</a>. The same trainer runs as a command-line app and in the browser, so your history file works in both.</p>
  <p>Read the <a href={ROUTES.privacy}>privacy page</a> for exactly what is stored, and the <a href={ROUTES.terms}>terms</a> for the rules of use.</p>
</LongForm>
